WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.922 --> 00:00:10.715
Christiana Figueres: There's no doubt

00:00:10.757 --> 00:00:14.594
that we are facing exponential
impacts of climate change.

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:18.139
There is also, however, no doubt

00:00:18.181 --> 00:00:23.270
that that is being met
with exponential progress

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:27.357
of the technologies that can help
to address climate change.

00:00:27.399 --> 00:00:33.446
So what we have here is, frankly,
a race between two exponential curves.

00:00:35.073 --> 00:00:36.825
By 2030,

00:00:36.867 --> 00:00:41.329
we collectively will have chosen
between door number one.

00:00:47.127 --> 00:00:48.545
And door number two.

00:00:54.718 --> 00:00:57.262
Julio Friedmann: The question
I am most commonly asked

00:00:57.262 --> 00:00:58.471
about climate change is:

00:00:58.513 --> 00:01:00.807
"Should I be optimistic or pessimistic?"

00:01:00.849 --> 00:01:03.518
Cynthia Williams: Here's what
we need to do to make sure

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.979
that we're ready
for an all-electric future.

00:01:06.021 --> 00:01:10.567
Anika Goss: Being financially secure
and climate resilient

00:01:10.609 --> 00:01:14.237
should be the most important priority.

00:01:14.279 --> 00:01:17.699
Faustine Delasalle: It's really easy
when you look at where we are today

00:01:17.699 --> 00:01:19.951
and where we ought to be by 2030,

00:01:19.951 --> 00:01:25.498
to feel like there's a huge gap
that will never be filled.

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:31.087
But we have good reasons to say
that that gap is actually fixable.

00:01:31.671 --> 00:01:33.924
Nigel Topping: If you're not
worried or anxious,

00:01:33.965 --> 00:01:35.759
you're probably not paying attention.

00:01:35.759 --> 00:01:37.552
But we should also not be defeatist.

00:01:37.552 --> 00:01:39.387
We shouldn’t say, “We’ve done nothing,

00:01:39.387 --> 00:01:40.680
we're failing."

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:42.933
We've really started 20 years too late,

00:01:42.974 --> 00:01:46.394
but it takes a long time to get
the flywheel of momentum going.

00:01:47.062 --> 00:01:49.272
Kingsmill Bond: The tide
of change is coming.

00:01:49.564 --> 00:01:51.066
The costs keep falling.

00:01:51.066 --> 00:01:53.526
The political pressure keeps increasing.

00:01:53.526 --> 00:01:57.072
JF: We're going to build
a thriving, exciting world.

00:01:57.113 --> 00:01:58.490
AG: We can do this.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:01:59.824
JF: Full of potential.

00:01:59.866 --> 00:02:01.242
AG: We have to do this.

00:02:03.244 --> 00:02:07.457
[TED Explores A New Climate Vision]

00:02:07.457 --> 00:02:09.834
Manoush Zamorodi:
When it comes to climate change,

00:02:09.834 --> 00:02:12.128
are you an optimist or a pessimist?

00:02:12.128 --> 00:02:16.716
News about the climate
can be overwhelming and emotional,

00:02:16.758 --> 00:02:19.427
to the point that many of us
just feel numb.

00:02:19.803 --> 00:02:23.056
I’m Manoush Zomorodi, a longtime
public media journalist

00:02:23.056 --> 00:02:25.392
and host of the TED Radio Hour.

00:02:25.392 --> 00:02:27.769
And today, we want
to bring you something special:

00:02:27.811 --> 00:02:30.689
an hour of nuance, of real people

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:34.526
and clarity about what
is happening to our planet

00:02:34.567 --> 00:02:36.778
and what we're doing about it.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:41.282
Because we all know there's plenty of bad
news when it comes to the climate crisis.

00:02:41.324 --> 00:02:42.492
But guess what?

00:02:42.492 --> 00:02:44.577
There's also some really good news.

00:02:45.954 --> 00:02:49.749
That's why we're here in Detroit
at the TED Countdown Summit,

00:02:49.749 --> 00:02:52.210
where a cross-section of people
who know the most

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:53.586
and who are doing the most

00:02:53.628 --> 00:02:57.298
have gathered to share their ideas
and plans with each other

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:58.591
and with you.

00:02:59.342 --> 00:03:01.678
You're going to hear
from some incredible speakers

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:04.514
about their personal stories
and tested solutions.

00:03:04.889 --> 00:03:08.601
But first, let's get a quick
reminder on the basics:

00:03:08.601 --> 00:03:11.438
climate change in a minute or so.

00:03:13.273 --> 00:03:18.069
[Prologue: What is Climate Change?]

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:20.989
David Biello: The air we
breathe has changed.

00:03:20.989 --> 00:03:23.158
The mix of gases is shifting,

00:03:23.199 --> 00:03:26.703
with more and more greenhouse gases
like carbon dioxide.

00:03:26.745 --> 00:03:29.914
And the shift is happening
faster each year.

00:03:29.956 --> 00:03:31.416
In just a few hundred years,

00:03:31.458 --> 00:03:37.088
fossil fuels formed over eons
have been burned as coal, oil and gas.

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:40.842
The exhaust has transformed
the entire atmosphere and ocean.

00:03:40.884 --> 00:03:42.343
It's like a pollution blanket.

00:03:42.385 --> 00:03:45.430
And the result we know as climate change.

00:03:45.472 --> 00:03:49.184
The planet has already warmed
more than one degree Celsius

00:03:49.184 --> 00:03:51.770
and is on a path to heat up even more.

00:03:51.811 --> 00:03:53.772
That may not seem like a lot,

00:03:53.813 --> 00:03:57.567
but it's already caused major
destruction across the globe.

00:03:57.567 --> 00:03:59.360
To give us some room to breathe,

00:03:59.402 --> 00:04:02.030
the world must reduce
greenhouse gas pollution

00:04:02.072 --> 00:04:04.616
by more than seven percent each year,

00:04:04.616 --> 00:04:06.701
every year of this decade.

00:04:07.368 --> 00:04:09.913
There are a number of ways to do that.

00:04:09.954 --> 00:04:13.500
Let's start by looking
at the greenhouse gases themselves.

00:04:13.541 --> 00:04:18.630
CO2 makes up nearly 75 percent
of the pollution emitted each year.

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:23.218
And then there's methane or natural gas,
which makes up 17 percent.

00:04:23.259 --> 00:04:27.680
Finally, there's nitrous oxide,
making up six percent of the problem.

00:04:27.722 --> 00:04:29.599
There are other greenhouse gases,

00:04:29.641 --> 00:04:32.560
but these three make up the bulk
of the climate challenge.

00:04:32.602 --> 00:04:34.104
Where do they come from?

00:04:34.479 --> 00:04:38.358
There are several ways to break down
the sources of greenhouse gas emissions.

00:04:38.358 --> 00:04:40.735
Here we’re using the public
Climate Watch data.

00:04:41.111 --> 00:04:42.445
Start with energy.

00:04:42.445 --> 00:04:46.282
The majority of modern energy
comes from burning fossil fuels,

00:04:46.324 --> 00:04:50.995
which makes the energy sector 76 percent
of the climate challenge,

00:04:51.037 --> 00:04:53.706
including the fuels
used for transportation,

00:04:53.706 --> 00:04:56.876
industrial processes
and agricultural production.

00:04:57.210 --> 00:04:59.963
Farming and animal raising
also have to change,

00:05:00.004 --> 00:05:03.591
as agriculture accounts for 12 percent
of greenhouse gas pollution.

00:05:04.134 --> 00:05:05.468
The remaining 12 percent

00:05:05.468 --> 00:05:09.806
comes from a grab bag of human activities
like industrial heating,

00:05:09.806 --> 00:05:12.016
clearing forests and more.

00:05:12.475 --> 00:05:15.270
This is the climate challenge we face:

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:19.107
going from adding billions
of metric tons of greenhouse gases

00:05:19.149 --> 00:05:22.861
to the air each year to adding zero.

00:05:23.486 --> 00:05:24.737
Will it be easy?

00:05:24.779 --> 00:05:25.905
No.

00:05:25.947 --> 00:05:27.115
Can we do it?

00:05:27.115 --> 00:05:28.867
Yes, if we choose to.

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:33.538
MZ: OK, so that's the situation.

00:05:33.872 --> 00:05:35.498
Where do we go from here?

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:37.750
Well, we've all heard of a tipping point,

00:05:37.792 --> 00:05:42.130
that moment when an idea
or concept finally takes hold

00:05:42.172 --> 00:05:43.882
and then changes the world.

00:05:43.882 --> 00:05:46.092
Well, the UN's climate chief,
Simon Stiell,

00:05:46.092 --> 00:05:51.556
believes we are almost at a tipping point
for massive action on climate change.

00:05:51.598 --> 00:05:54.934
And to get you ready, he wants
to take you back to the early '90s,

00:05:54.976 --> 00:05:57.437
when he was an executive at Nokia,

00:05:57.437 --> 00:06:01.441
and the world wasn't sending
text messages yet.

00:06:02.734 --> 00:06:06.279
[Chapter 1: Exponential Change]

00:06:07.822 --> 00:06:11.534
Simon Stiell: I want you
to imagine it’s 1992,

00:06:11.576 --> 00:06:14.245
and you're talking to a telecoms expert.

00:06:14.954 --> 00:06:18.333
She tells you that mobile
phone text messaging

00:06:18.374 --> 00:06:22.587
is going to fundamentally change
the way we communicate.

00:06:23.087 --> 00:06:27.217
You think, why on Earth
am I going to go from this

00:06:27.217 --> 00:06:31.262
to tapping on a screen
with my fingers and my thumbs?

00:06:31.638 --> 00:06:35.642
But then, after a slow start,

00:06:35.683 --> 00:06:39.145
text-based services exploded.

00:06:39.145 --> 00:06:46.152
Today, over 23 billion messages
are sent every single day.

00:06:46.527 --> 00:06:50.782
This paved the way
for the smartphone revolution.

00:06:50.823 --> 00:06:55.245
I know how much we struggle to comprehend

00:06:55.245 --> 00:07:00.291
and predict what exponential change means,

00:07:00.333 --> 00:07:05.463
because I was at Nokia in 1993

00:07:05.463 --> 00:07:08.716
when that technological
revolution started.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:13.471
And now, as head of UN climate change,

00:07:13.513 --> 00:07:17.475
I'm here to tell you that what
was true for mobile phones

00:07:17.475 --> 00:07:21.437
is also true for climate action today.

00:07:21.938 --> 00:07:27.277
We're here in Detroit,
the heart of car manufacturing.

00:07:27.902 --> 00:07:30.071
Take a look at this graph.

00:07:30.113 --> 00:07:32.657
Electric vehicle sales

00:07:32.657 --> 00:07:38.913
are expected to increase
to become 70 percent by 2030.

00:07:39.622 --> 00:07:43.209
Another example, solar energy.

00:07:43.251 --> 00:07:47.171
Between 2010 and 2020,

00:07:47.213 --> 00:07:52.969
we moved from 20 gigawatts
of solar energy installed

00:07:53.011 --> 00:07:55.555
to 150 gigawatts.

00:07:55.596 --> 00:08:00.852
And by 2030, that number
is expected to increase again,

00:08:00.852 --> 00:08:04.731
to 1,000 gigawatts per year.

00:08:05.398 --> 00:08:08.026
Change can come fast.

00:08:09.944 --> 00:08:15.867
FD: So exponential change means
that change happens very slowly at first

00:08:15.908 --> 00:08:18.244
and then comes a tipping point.

00:08:18.286 --> 00:08:21.622
And suddenly things accelerate
and accelerate and accelerate.

00:08:21.664 --> 00:08:23.082
NT: It's a mathematical term,

00:08:23.082 --> 00:08:25.335
but it means basically
stuff happens very slowly

00:08:25.335 --> 00:08:26.753
and then it goes really fast.

00:08:26.753 --> 00:08:32.342
Tessa Kahn: Governments and experts
have consistently underestimated the pace

00:08:32.383 --> 00:08:35.636
and scale at which we can
deploy renewable energy

00:08:35.636 --> 00:08:37.430
and how quickly renewable energy

00:08:37.430 --> 00:08:40.516
and associated technologies
become affordable.

00:08:40.558 --> 00:08:42.810
Ramez Naam: I predicted in 2011

00:08:42.852 --> 00:08:47.357
that we'd have solar as cheap as coal
in some parts of the world by 2015

00:08:47.398 --> 00:08:50.693
and half the cost of coal by 2020,

00:08:50.735 --> 00:08:52.862
and everyone thought I was crazy,

00:08:52.904 --> 00:08:54.864
I've got to say, almost.

00:08:54.864 --> 00:08:56.741
And in fact, I was wrong.

00:08:57.325 --> 00:08:59.786
The actual decline in the cost of solar

00:08:59.827 --> 00:09:02.705
happened twice as fast
as I thought it would.

00:09:03.414 --> 00:09:08.002
Solar prices today are a century ahead

00:09:08.002 --> 00:09:12.840
of where the world's leading energy
experts thought they'd be in 2010.

00:09:12.882 --> 00:09:18.388
KB: It's now spreading from solar,
so it's happening also in the wind sector,

00:09:18.429 --> 00:09:20.264
it's happening in the battery sector,

00:09:20.264 --> 00:09:22.433
it's happening in the electric
vehicle sector.

00:09:22.475 --> 00:09:27.605
CF: I don't think that we do
a good job at communicating the fact

00:09:27.605 --> 00:09:33.111
that we are progressing
more than is commonly known.

00:09:33.152 --> 00:09:36.656
Nili Gilbert: The human brain
is trained best

00:09:36.697 --> 00:09:39.492
at thinking about linear change.

00:09:39.492 --> 00:09:42.203
But when you think
about an exponential curve,

00:09:42.203 --> 00:09:43.663
next to a line,

00:09:43.663 --> 00:09:46.749
at first the exponential curve
is below the line,

00:09:46.749 --> 00:09:49.210
and so it's moving more slowly
than you would think.

00:09:49.252 --> 00:09:50.628
And then it crosses the line

00:09:50.628 --> 00:09:52.713
and it's moving faster
than you would think.

00:09:52.713 --> 00:09:54.841
Habiba Daggash: We call it
cautious optimism.

00:09:54.841 --> 00:09:56.008
We're very hopeful

00:09:56.050 --> 00:09:57.927
about the trajectories that we've seen.

00:09:57.969 --> 00:09:59.929
But we shouldn't let up yet.

00:09:59.971 --> 00:10:03.433
KB: So we can make this change happen slow
or we can make it happen fast.

00:10:03.433 --> 00:10:05.309
The key point to say is we have agency.

00:10:09.021 --> 00:10:12.525
MZ: This idea of exponential
growth sounds awesome.

00:10:12.567 --> 00:10:16.070
It sounds amazing,
but is it actually happening?

00:10:16.112 --> 00:10:19.782
Well, I'm headed into a workshop
with industry and climate leaders

00:10:19.824 --> 00:10:20.950
to find out.

00:10:23.286 --> 00:10:27.790
The moderators of the workshop
challenged us to hold two competing ideas

00:10:27.832 --> 00:10:29.876
in our minds at the same time.

00:10:29.917 --> 00:10:32.003
NT: What we're really
going to explore today

00:10:32.003 --> 00:10:33.796
is two apparently opposing realities,

00:10:33.796 --> 00:10:36.257
and they're often two opposing narratives.

00:10:36.257 --> 00:10:40.428
The one which is true, right,
is that in terms of the Paris goals,

00:10:40.428 --> 00:10:41.679
we're way off track.

00:10:42.430 --> 00:10:43.931
We're deep into the emergency.

00:10:43.973 --> 00:10:46.726
But what we're going to explore today
is the other reality

00:10:46.726 --> 00:10:48.144
that we know what to do.

00:10:48.186 --> 00:10:52.315
Exponential change is happening,
but it doesn't happen by magic.

00:10:52.356 --> 00:10:54.901
MZ: So what are we seeing
in different industries?

00:10:54.901 --> 00:10:57.904
Well, let's start with one
that's known to be really difficult

00:10:57.904 --> 00:10:59.989
to decarbonize: cement.

00:10:59.989 --> 00:11:03.868
Vicente Saiso Alva: I think everything
converges into making climate

00:11:03.910 --> 00:11:07.413
a huge urgent matter for society.

00:11:07.413 --> 00:11:12.585
We started to feel the pressure
from all different types of stakeholders,

00:11:12.627 --> 00:11:15.588
and in one year of running this program,

00:11:15.588 --> 00:11:18.257
we realized how fast we could move

00:11:18.299 --> 00:11:22.678
and how fast we were going
in reducing CO2 emissions,

00:11:22.678 --> 00:11:29.018
which means now to go from 40
to close to 50 percent reduction by 2030.

00:11:30.228 --> 00:11:33.189
MZ: Encouraging, but to tackle
that remaining 50 percent

00:11:33.231 --> 00:11:37.735
may require technologies
that don't exist at scale yet.

00:11:37.777 --> 00:11:40.112
OK, what about shipping?

00:11:40.154 --> 00:11:42.073
Narin Phol: In Maersk, a few years back,

00:11:42.073 --> 00:11:45.993
we came out with a net-zero
ambition by 2050,

00:11:46.035 --> 00:11:48.996
and in a few years later,
based on the data that we have got,

00:11:49.038 --> 00:11:52.041
we've really seen a path
that we can really accelerate that one.

00:11:52.041 --> 00:11:54.710
That's why the revised target
of 2040 came in.

00:11:54.752 --> 00:11:58.714
MZ: The news in offshore wind
was quite dramatic.

00:11:58.714 --> 00:12:03.177
Jennie Dodson: So my moment
of mindset shift was in 2019.

00:12:03.219 --> 00:12:07.890
The government put out a request
to bid for offshore wind permits.

00:12:07.932 --> 00:12:12.478
And that year the price that came in
was two thirds less

00:12:12.478 --> 00:12:14.564
than just four years before.

00:12:14.564 --> 00:12:15.982
And really critically,

00:12:16.023 --> 00:12:19.944
it was the same price
as the mainstream market.

00:12:19.986 --> 00:12:21.362
And that was transformative.

00:12:21.404 --> 00:12:23.781
Nobody expected that when they started.

00:12:23.823 --> 00:12:27.451
MZ: The conversation got pretty
technical and a bit wonky.

00:12:27.493 --> 00:12:29.662
NP: So to drive exponential growth,

00:12:29.704 --> 00:12:32.873
you focus on a reinforcing loop
to make sure it works

00:12:32.873 --> 00:12:35.042
and you slow down on the balancing loop.

00:12:35.042 --> 00:12:37.503
MZ: But the takeaway is
that certain sectors,

00:12:37.503 --> 00:12:41.340
like EVs and renewables,
are being upended,

00:12:41.382 --> 00:12:43.801
while others, like cement
and heavy industry

00:12:43.801 --> 00:12:45.261
are further behind.

00:12:45.303 --> 00:12:47.888
But generally across every industry --

00:12:47.930 --> 00:12:49.348
JD: It’s amazing stuff.

00:12:49.390 --> 00:12:53.352
MZ: There's an understanding
that they all need to work together.

00:12:53.394 --> 00:12:54.687
And the message?

00:12:54.729 --> 00:12:56.647
Big change can happen.

00:12:56.689 --> 00:13:00.192
It's going to be hard, but some of us
are already doing it,

00:13:00.234 --> 00:13:01.736
so learn from us.

00:13:03.654 --> 00:13:08.868
[Chapter 2: The Electric
Vehicle Revolution]

00:13:09.327 --> 00:13:12.997
OK, we are in an exponential mindset.

00:13:13.039 --> 00:13:14.665
And as you heard,

00:13:14.707 --> 00:13:16.917
one example of exponential growth

00:13:16.917 --> 00:13:19.837
might be sitting
in your driveway right now.

00:13:19.837 --> 00:13:23.633
Electric vehicles are finally
really happening,

00:13:23.633 --> 00:13:28.012
which means that people here in Detroit
are rethinking the auto industry.

00:13:28.054 --> 00:13:31.057
They're rethinking
their jobs, their identity.

00:13:31.057 --> 00:13:34.435
Take Cynthia Williams,
head of sustainability at Ford.

00:13:37.396 --> 00:13:41.150
I sat down with her at Lucky Detroit,
a local coffee and barber shop,

00:13:41.150 --> 00:13:44.320
where she told me that there are three
things that need to happen

00:13:44.362 --> 00:13:47.239
for electric vehicles to go
to the next level,

00:13:47.239 --> 00:13:50.743
from this tipping point
to exponential growth.

00:13:51.202 --> 00:13:53.537
CW: The three things for me are change,

00:13:53.579 --> 00:13:55.581
collaboration and capacity.

00:13:55.623 --> 00:14:00.920
So the change piece of it is changing
the way folks think about the vehicle,

00:14:00.920 --> 00:14:05.633
making sure that they understand
we're bringing a better vehicle

00:14:05.633 --> 00:14:06.842
to the customers.

00:14:06.842 --> 00:14:08.886
MZ: So you don't have to make a sacrifice.

00:14:08.928 --> 00:14:11.514
CW: I think one of the things
we need to do is actually

00:14:11.514 --> 00:14:12.848
get people in the cars

00:14:12.848 --> 00:14:15.643
and show them how fun it is.

00:14:15.643 --> 00:14:18.354
And it's no different,
it's just powered by a battery.

00:14:18.354 --> 00:14:20.481
We are at a tipping point.

00:14:20.481 --> 00:14:23.943
We're at a point of acceleration
of electric vehicles.

00:14:23.984 --> 00:14:28.197
Many are calling it
the electric vehicle revolution.

00:14:28.197 --> 00:14:31.575
Now with that comes unprecedented change

00:14:31.617 --> 00:14:34.495
that will require us to build new capacity

00:14:34.495 --> 00:14:38.416
and collaborate in ways
that we've never seen before.

00:14:38.457 --> 00:14:41.794
To make things happen faster,
you have to think differently.

00:14:42.461 --> 00:14:45.464
You can't just collaborate
with the same people

00:14:45.464 --> 00:14:47.341
because you get the same answer, right?

00:14:47.383 --> 00:14:50.052
You need to broaden
your collaboration space.

00:14:50.094 --> 00:14:51.512
Go outside of your industry,

00:14:51.554 --> 00:14:55.683
understand what worked
in Norway, for example,

00:14:55.725 --> 00:14:57.184
to get them to where they are.

00:14:57.226 --> 00:14:59.061
MZ: Where's the weak link, Cynthia?

00:14:59.103 --> 00:15:02.857
What's the thing that you're like,
that if we don't push through that,

00:15:02.898 --> 00:15:05.651
we may not get to this full adoption?

00:15:05.693 --> 00:15:08.320
CW: The critical things
that we hear from the consumers

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:10.197
is they need infrastructure.

00:15:10.197 --> 00:15:12.950
That's what it's taking for us
to work with governments

00:15:12.992 --> 00:15:17.413
to make sure that we bring
more charging to everyone,

00:15:17.413 --> 00:15:19.790
whether it's rural, urban,

00:15:19.790 --> 00:15:22.334
wealthy, low-income, we all need chargers.

00:15:22.334 --> 00:15:24.545
And I'm not saying we need one
on every corner,

00:15:24.587 --> 00:15:26.630
but we do need many, many more.

00:15:26.630 --> 00:15:30.426
When we start building out the charging
infrastructure, we need lighting.

00:15:30.426 --> 00:15:34.180
We need, you know, security,
shelter, we need restrooms.

00:15:34.221 --> 00:15:35.681
We need other amenities

00:15:35.723 --> 00:15:38.809
so that people know
where to go, where to charge.

00:15:39.393 --> 00:15:41.270
Next comes capacity.

00:15:41.270 --> 00:15:46.567
We have to invest
in new facilities and talent.

00:15:46.609 --> 00:15:51.197
Now, that's true for any industry
transitioning to greener goals

00:15:51.238 --> 00:15:52.656
and strategies.

00:15:52.990 --> 00:15:55.576
And we're investing in talent,

00:15:55.576 --> 00:15:58.412
we're investing in education and training,

00:15:58.454 --> 00:16:01.457
and we're also listening
to the communities

00:16:01.499 --> 00:16:03.209
where we live and operate.

00:16:03.209 --> 00:16:05.586
MZ: So a young Cynthia Williams, now,

00:16:05.628 --> 00:16:08.631
your counterpart
who's studying engineering,

00:16:08.672 --> 00:16:12.218
will she be learning
something very different

00:16:12.218 --> 00:16:15.346
if she wants to go and work
in the automotive industry in Detroit?

00:16:15.387 --> 00:16:17.389
CW: There will be
different options, right.

00:16:17.389 --> 00:16:20.226
And so, I'm a mechanical
engineer by trade,

00:16:20.267 --> 00:16:24.063
but I think there will be opportunities
for electrical engineering,

00:16:24.104 --> 00:16:25.481
software engineering.

00:16:25.481 --> 00:16:28.526
And there's even certificates
that students can get

00:16:28.567 --> 00:16:30.694
instead of going through
a four-year college.

00:16:30.694 --> 00:16:33.697
It's the proper training to get
them right, to be dedicated,

00:16:33.739 --> 00:16:36.075
to build the vehicles
that we need for the future.

00:16:36.116 --> 00:16:38.035
At the end of this year, globally,

00:16:38.077 --> 00:16:42.248
we’ll have the ability to produce
600,000 electric vehicles.

00:16:42.289 --> 00:16:48.754
And in 2026, we’ll have a global goal
to produce two million electric vehicles.

00:16:48.796 --> 00:16:50.673
And it just goes up from there.

00:16:50.673 --> 00:16:52.424
MZ: What is this going to look like,

00:16:52.424 --> 00:16:56.136
this growth for me in the next couple
of years, in five years, in 20 years?

00:16:56.136 --> 00:16:57.513
CW: Clean air.

00:16:58.138 --> 00:16:59.682
That's what I look at.

00:16:59.682 --> 00:17:02.643
If you think about when we
were, during the pandemic,

00:17:02.643 --> 00:17:06.438
when everybody stopped driving
and they were at home,

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:11.110
clear skies and moving to zero
tailpipe emission vehicles.

00:17:11.151 --> 00:17:13.404
And if you chose an electric vehicle,

00:17:13.445 --> 00:17:15.281
that's you participating

00:17:15.322 --> 00:17:17.741
and you contributing to a better world,

00:17:17.783 --> 00:17:19.285
to a better future.

00:17:19.285 --> 00:17:21.036
We have come a long way.

00:17:21.036 --> 00:17:24.832
It's so gratifying to see
electric vehicles on the road,

00:17:24.874 --> 00:17:28.460
to move from aspiration to reality.

00:17:28.794 --> 00:17:33.591
To see plants dedicated
to building electric vehicles.

00:17:33.632 --> 00:17:35.926
We have the technology,

00:17:35.968 --> 00:17:37.386
we have the leadership,

00:17:37.428 --> 00:17:44.018
we have the ingenuity to respond
to the environmental crisis with courage.

00:17:44.518 --> 00:17:48.814
And I'll tell you, as a person
who's dedicated my entire career

00:17:48.814 --> 00:17:50.608
to sustainability,

00:17:50.649 --> 00:17:54.320
the road ahead is paved with promise.

00:17:54.361 --> 00:17:57.364
(Applause)

00:17:59.074 --> 00:18:02.119
TED-Ed: If you were
buying a car in 1899,

00:18:02.119 --> 00:18:05.539
you would have had three major
options to choose from.

00:18:05.581 --> 00:18:07.541
You could buy a steam-powered car,

00:18:07.583 --> 00:18:10.127
typically relying on gas-powered boilers.

00:18:10.169 --> 00:18:12.379
These could drive as far as you wanted,

00:18:12.379 --> 00:18:16.467
provided you also wanted to lug
around extra water to refuel

00:18:16.467 --> 00:18:20.304
and didn't mind waiting 30 minutes
for your engine to heat up.

00:18:20.346 --> 00:18:24.141
Alternatively, you could buy
a car powered by gasoline.

00:18:24.183 --> 00:18:27.102
However, the internal combustion
engines in these models

00:18:27.102 --> 00:18:30.189
required dangerous hand cranking to start

00:18:30.189 --> 00:18:34.568
and emitted loud noises
and foul smelling exhaust while driving.

00:18:35.152 --> 00:18:38.197
So your best bet was probably
option number three:

00:18:38.197 --> 00:18:41.367
a battery-powered electric vehicle.

00:18:41.367 --> 00:18:45.204
These cars were quick to start,
clean and quiet to run,

00:18:45.204 --> 00:18:48.207
and if you lived somewhere
with access to electricity,

00:18:48.248 --> 00:18:50.793
easy to refuel overnight.

00:18:50.793 --> 00:18:54.672
If this seems like an easy choice,
you're not alone.

00:18:54.713 --> 00:18:56.423
By the end of the 19th century,

00:18:56.465 --> 00:19:00.302
nearly 40 percent
of American cars were electric.

00:19:00.678 --> 00:19:03.180
In cities with early electric systems,

00:19:03.222 --> 00:19:07.184
battery-powered cars were a popular
and reliable alternative

00:19:07.184 --> 00:19:10.396
to their occasionally
explosive competitors.

00:19:10.437 --> 00:19:13.440
But electric vehicles
had one major problem.

00:19:13.941 --> 00:19:15.109
Batteries.

00:19:17.152 --> 00:19:19.029
MZ: Flash forward to the present

00:19:19.029 --> 00:19:21.323
and batteries are still
on everyone's mind.

00:19:21.323 --> 00:19:23.200
So I visited Mujeeb Ijaz,

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:26.120
the founder of the energy
storage company One,

00:19:26.161 --> 00:19:29.873
who also happens to be
an electric vehicle history buff.

00:19:30.499 --> 00:19:33.794
Mujeeb Ijaz: So this
is a 1922 Detroit Electric.

00:19:34.128 --> 00:19:36.338
MZ: OK, so 100-year-old vehicle.

00:19:36.338 --> 00:19:38.090
MI: That's right, electric vehicle.

00:19:38.132 --> 00:19:40.634
And then this was Walter Baker.

00:19:40.634 --> 00:19:42.469
He developed the Baker Electric.

00:19:42.469 --> 00:19:46.890
And he made the range run
201 miles on a single charge.

00:19:46.932 --> 00:19:50.561
He also felt strongly that you should
be able to wear your top hat, you know,

00:19:50.602 --> 00:19:52.980
so he has a taller carriage.

00:19:53.022 --> 00:19:55.357
These had lead acid
batteries to begin with.

00:19:55.399 --> 00:19:58.527
And then Thomas Edison
introduced the nickel-iron battery.

00:19:59.111 --> 00:20:02.948
So this is the battery compartment,
one of the battery compartments.

00:20:02.948 --> 00:20:04.700
And this is where you would charge.

00:20:04.700 --> 00:20:05.868
MZ: Right there?

00:20:05.909 --> 00:20:09.038
MI: And we're building
on what these original pioneers did.

00:20:09.538 --> 00:20:13.500
And I think it's important to not think
about us starting from scratch now.

00:20:13.834 --> 00:20:15.002
But go backwards,

00:20:15.044 --> 00:20:16.962
think about how they solved the problem,

00:20:16.962 --> 00:20:20.174
what disrupted their ability
to advance this to the market,

00:20:20.215 --> 00:20:23.802
and then pick those problems up
and make sure we don't get stuck again.

00:20:23.844 --> 00:20:25.429
MZ: So we've been here before,

00:20:25.471 --> 00:20:30.142
a moment when electric vehicles
could have taken off but didn't.

00:20:30.184 --> 00:20:33.645
So what do we need to do
differently this time?

00:20:33.687 --> 00:20:37.733
MI: Electric vehicles were able to deal
with mobility in urban areas,

00:20:37.775 --> 00:20:39.318
but there were charging deserts

00:20:39.318 --> 00:20:43.197
where rural driving resulted
in no way to get back

00:20:43.238 --> 00:20:46.158
because you couldn't find
electric charging everywhere.

00:20:46.158 --> 00:20:47.326
And that was a problem.

00:20:47.326 --> 00:20:49.912
MZ: And that's what people
are worried about right now.

00:20:49.953 --> 00:20:51.121
We're at the same thing.

00:20:51.121 --> 00:20:53.999
We're worried about range,
we're worried about accessibility.

00:20:54.041 --> 00:20:55.542
All of the things are the same.

00:20:55.542 --> 00:20:57.711
But the key difference being
that we now know

00:20:57.711 --> 00:21:01.090
that our planet
can't keep going like this.

00:21:01.090 --> 00:21:02.299
MI: That's right.

00:21:02.299 --> 00:21:05.928
Had the vision
of the Model T assembly line

00:21:05.928 --> 00:21:10.516
and mass manufacturing
been applied to electric car,

00:21:10.516 --> 00:21:13.185
the electric car would have
also dropped in price,

00:21:13.227 --> 00:21:17.898
but range and charging deserts
would have still been the obstruction.

00:21:17.898 --> 00:21:19.066
MZ: OK.

00:21:19.525 --> 00:21:22.069
We didn't have the technology,
but we do now.

00:21:22.111 --> 00:21:23.403
MI: We do now.

00:21:24.238 --> 00:21:29.326
MZ: In the US, there hasn’t been
a mass move to electric vehicles ... yet.

00:21:29.993 --> 00:21:33.789
Mujeeb's company is one of many who want
to change that by building batteries

00:21:33.789 --> 00:21:36.291
that give drivers longer range.

00:21:36.291 --> 00:21:39.128
And these batteries aren't just for cars.

00:21:39.169 --> 00:21:43.549
MI: So we produce battery packs
for transportation,

00:21:43.590 --> 00:21:46.051
but we've also started developing

00:21:46.051 --> 00:21:48.762
the same battery products
into grid batteries.

00:21:49.221 --> 00:21:54.184
So micro-grids are those technologies
that combine solar and storage

00:21:54.184 --> 00:21:57.646
and replace a coal plant
or a natural gas plant

00:21:57.688 --> 00:21:59.189
for generating electricity.

00:21:59.231 --> 00:22:01.942
So buildings and industry

00:22:01.984 --> 00:22:04.570
and transportation all working together.

00:22:04.611 --> 00:22:08.031
And that's an exciting future, is all
these industries working together,

00:22:08.031 --> 00:22:09.408
that makes me more confident

00:22:09.408 --> 00:22:12.828
that we're at the time in history
where this transition will take place.

00:22:12.828 --> 00:22:14.705
MZ: How many years till we get to that?

00:22:14.705 --> 00:22:16.290
MI: I think it's within 10 years.

00:22:16.331 --> 00:22:17.666
MZ: No way, 10 years?

00:22:17.708 --> 00:22:22.129
MI: We're in a place where that transition
is going to be much more visible,

00:22:22.171 --> 00:22:26.091
and then we just need technologies
to drive it up to the mass markets.

00:22:28.218 --> 00:22:29.636
MZ: I am so excited.

00:22:29.678 --> 00:22:31.680
Like, genuinely so excited.

00:22:32.472 --> 00:22:36.101
Everyone everywhere
driving electric sounds great,

00:22:36.143 --> 00:22:39.646
but that also means that the power
to charge all those vehicles

00:22:39.688 --> 00:22:41.523
needs to be green.

00:22:41.523 --> 00:22:44.276
And that brings us to the energy sector,

00:22:44.276 --> 00:22:47.529
which is also changing
at an incredible pace,

00:22:47.571 --> 00:22:51.200
even though renewable energy
has been years in the making.

00:22:53.577 --> 00:22:56.455
[Chapter 3: Renewables]

00:22:58.498 --> 00:23:02.419
TED-Ed: In the spring of 1954,
the press excitedly gathered

00:23:02.419 --> 00:23:05.714
around Bell Laboratories’
latest invention:

00:23:05.714 --> 00:23:09.343
a silicon-based solar cell
that could efficiently convert

00:23:09.384 --> 00:23:12.221
the sun's energy into electrical current.

00:23:13.055 --> 00:23:17.434
The creation was celebrated
as the dawn of a new era,

00:23:17.434 --> 00:23:18.644
as reporters touted

00:23:18.685 --> 00:23:23.232
that civilization would soon run
on the sun's limitless energy.

00:23:23.273 --> 00:23:25.150
But the dream had a catch.

00:23:25.150 --> 00:23:31.531
As this first commercially sold solar cell
cost around 300 dollars per watt,

00:23:31.531 --> 00:23:36.495
meaning at its current rate it would cost
well over a million dollars

00:23:36.536 --> 00:23:40.540
to buy a unit large enough
to power a single home.

00:23:40.540 --> 00:23:42.334
But today, in many countries,

00:23:42.376 --> 00:23:45.420
solar is the cheapest form
of energy to produce,

00:23:45.462 --> 00:23:49.758
surpassing fossil fuel alternatives
like coal and natural gas.

00:23:50.217 --> 00:23:53.262
Millions of homes are equipped
with rooftop solar,

00:23:53.262 --> 00:23:57.516
with most units paying for themselves
in their first seven to 12 years

00:23:57.516 --> 00:23:59.893
and then generating further savings.

00:24:00.560 --> 00:24:04.523
So how did solar become so affordable?

00:24:05.148 --> 00:24:07.276
A turning point in solar's price history

00:24:07.317 --> 00:24:10.445
occurred on the floor
of Germany's parliament,

00:24:10.487 --> 00:24:15.951
where in 2000, Hermann Scheer introduced
the Renewable Energy Sources Act.

00:24:16.451 --> 00:24:19.955
This legislation laid out a vision
for the country's energy future

00:24:19.955 --> 00:24:21.873
in solar and wind.

00:24:21.915 --> 00:24:26.795
It incentivized citizens to personally
invest in rooftop solar panels

00:24:26.837 --> 00:24:31.174
by guaranteeing payment to homeowners
for the renewable energy they generated

00:24:31.216 --> 00:24:33.010
and sold to the grid.

00:24:33.510 --> 00:24:36.513
The pay rate for this electricity
was highly subsidized,

00:24:36.555 --> 00:24:39.766
at times reaching four times
the market price.

00:24:39.808 --> 00:24:43.312
Several other countries soon
followed Germany's example,

00:24:43.312 --> 00:24:46.023
implementing similar
policies and incentives

00:24:46.064 --> 00:24:48.775
to drive their country's solar use.

00:24:49.651 --> 00:24:53.822
This created unprecedented demand
for solar panels worldwide.

00:24:53.822 --> 00:24:56.158
Manufacturers were able
to scale up production

00:24:56.199 --> 00:24:58.744
and innovate in ways that cut costs.

00:24:58.785 --> 00:25:03.081
As a result, solar panel prices
dropped while efficiency grew.

00:25:04.916 --> 00:25:07.044
HD: We see the cost reduction in solar

00:25:07.085 --> 00:25:10.839
and other renewable energy
technologies has been so steep,

00:25:10.839 --> 00:25:15.010
but a lot of it is because of the enabling
regulatory and policy environments

00:25:15.010 --> 00:25:17.971
created in places like
the European Union and the US.

00:25:17.971 --> 00:25:21.350
Akil Callender: We cannot divert
catastrophic climate change

00:25:21.391 --> 00:25:23.518
without addressing emissions

00:25:23.518 --> 00:25:25.562
coming from our energy systems.

00:25:25.604 --> 00:25:27.689
Currently, emissions
from the energy system

00:25:27.731 --> 00:25:31.568
account for around two thirds
of global greenhouse gas emissions.

00:25:31.610 --> 00:25:35.864
So the good news is, yes,
we are seeing tremendous progress

00:25:35.864 --> 00:25:37.657
across the globe in renewable energy.

00:25:37.657 --> 00:25:39.826
It's rolling out faster than ever before.

00:25:39.826 --> 00:25:43.038
Luisa Neubauer: The brilliant thing
about energy supply in the world

00:25:43.080 --> 00:25:44.498
is that we have alternatives.

00:25:44.498 --> 00:25:45.916
So we don't need fossil fuels

00:25:45.916 --> 00:25:51.713
to provide safe and equitable
access to energy systems.

00:25:51.755 --> 00:25:55.425
Ramón Méndez Galain: Almost 90 percent
of the total new installed capacity

00:25:55.425 --> 00:25:57.219
all over the world is just renewable.

00:25:57.260 --> 00:25:59.721
This is a tremendous change.

00:25:59.721 --> 00:26:03.100
AC: There's more finance than ever before
going into clean energy.

00:26:03.141 --> 00:26:07.813
In many countries, it is the largest
contributor to their electricity system

00:26:07.854 --> 00:26:09.022
for the first time ever.

00:26:09.022 --> 00:26:12.609
KB: And also because China is the leading
manufacturing nation in the world,

00:26:12.651 --> 00:26:14.486
change is happening there much faster.

00:26:14.528 --> 00:26:17.280
And those innovations
are then spreading around the world.

00:26:17.280 --> 00:26:19.991
Hongquiao Liu: China's coal demand
might not have peaked,

00:26:19.991 --> 00:26:23.662
and China will continue to consume
quite a lot of coal in the next few years.

00:26:23.662 --> 00:26:25.956
But the decarbonization is happening.

00:26:25.997 --> 00:26:29.167
It's happening faster
than anyone has expected,

00:26:29.209 --> 00:26:33.505
and China is adding an astonishing
amount of wind and solar

00:26:33.547 --> 00:26:35.132
to its grid every year.

00:26:35.132 --> 00:26:37.884
Eduarda Zoghbi: I think one
of the biggest obstacles today

00:26:37.926 --> 00:26:39.094
are natural resources,

00:26:39.094 --> 00:26:43.140
in the sense that we're talking more
and more about critical minerals

00:26:43.181 --> 00:26:44.558
and the role that they have.

00:26:44.558 --> 00:26:47.102
And batteries are demanding
a lot of critical minerals

00:26:47.102 --> 00:26:49.729
that are present in most
of the developing countries.

00:26:49.729 --> 00:26:52.107
So I think that even though
costs are decreasing,

00:26:52.149 --> 00:26:56.319
we have to be more and more creative
when it comes to recycling the batteries.

00:26:56.319 --> 00:26:59.865
We also have to think of how
they're impacting local communities.

00:26:59.865 --> 00:27:02.784
KB: So to give you the numbers,
we extract, at the moment,

00:27:02.826 --> 00:27:06.621
15,000 million tons
of fossil fuels every year.

00:27:06.621 --> 00:27:13.044
The International Energy Agency says
that we will need about 43 million tons

00:27:13.044 --> 00:27:14.629
of critical materials

00:27:14.629 --> 00:27:17.174
in order to build out
the clean energy economy.

00:27:17.174 --> 00:27:21.720
So that’s 300 times less
stuff that is required.

00:27:21.720 --> 00:27:23.805
It's worth saying that the impact

00:27:23.805 --> 00:27:29.269
of extracting 300 times
less stuff upon nature

00:27:29.269 --> 00:27:30.937
is dramatically lower.

00:27:30.979 --> 00:27:32.898
And I know that it's often argued

00:27:32.898 --> 00:27:35.901
that the renewable economy
may be damaging for nature.

00:27:35.901 --> 00:27:38.612
Again, this is a completely
false statement.

00:27:38.612 --> 00:27:44.159
It's in fact, our only chance of reducing
the pressure upon our natural resources,

00:27:44.201 --> 00:27:45.702
because there's far less of it.

00:27:47.245 --> 00:27:52.083
MZ: Of course, transitioning to using
clean energy, upending industries,

00:27:52.083 --> 00:27:55.462
it has to happen across the world,
not just here in the US.

00:27:55.504 --> 00:27:59.132
And it can be hard to even imagine
what life will be like.

00:27:59.174 --> 00:28:03.345
But carbon scientist and futurist
Julio Friedmann can help us.

00:28:06.640 --> 00:28:09.809
Julio Friedmann: I think we need
a compelling vision of the future

00:28:09.809 --> 00:28:12.312
that gets people excited
about the energy transition

00:28:12.312 --> 00:28:13.647
and solving climate change.

00:28:13.647 --> 00:28:15.690
Right now, it's like we're at a restaurant

00:28:15.732 --> 00:28:18.443
and there's two options:
burnt toast, unflavored oatmeal.

00:28:18.443 --> 00:28:20.695
And people are not excited
about either, right?

00:28:20.695 --> 00:28:23.698
The burnt toast option for me
is what I call the big switch.

00:28:23.698 --> 00:28:25.742
Everything's the same, it's just clean,

00:28:25.784 --> 00:28:28.495
but it costs a lot of money
and it's super hard to do.

00:28:28.537 --> 00:28:31.831
The other vision is kind of
like, “Thou shalt not.”

00:28:31.831 --> 00:28:37.754
That vision is a narrower sort of,
“eat your peas,” hair shirt

00:28:37.754 --> 00:28:39.548
kind of vision of the future.

00:28:39.589 --> 00:28:41.967
And a lot of people
don't want that either.

00:28:41.967 --> 00:28:44.636
And there's nothing in the physics,
chemistry or biology

00:28:44.678 --> 00:28:47.597
that says we can't have
a super interesting, exciting,

00:28:47.597 --> 00:28:48.890
vibrant world.

00:28:48.890 --> 00:28:53.103
And that is compelling in a way
that we have not heard yet.

00:28:53.687 --> 00:28:56.147
MZ: Help me out here,
what does that even look like?

00:28:56.189 --> 00:28:59.943
JF: So, for lack of a better word, like,
I like the Marvel cinematic universe.

00:28:59.985 --> 00:29:01.403
MZ: OK, I'll go with you.

00:29:01.444 --> 00:29:03.822
JF: So a place like Wakanda, right?

00:29:03.863 --> 00:29:06.700
It's got flying cars,
it's got maglev trains,

00:29:06.741 --> 00:29:09.494
and it's a totally fabulous place to live.

00:29:09.536 --> 00:29:11.037
Like, they've got lots of food.

00:29:11.037 --> 00:29:13.915
That is built on abundant,
sustainable, cheap energy.

00:29:13.957 --> 00:29:18.795
MZ: OK, so like, we're not Wakanda,
we're not in the Marvel universe.

00:29:18.837 --> 00:29:21.464
This is real life, sort of.

00:29:22.591 --> 00:29:24.050
How do we make this possible?

00:29:24.092 --> 00:29:26.928
JF: So what can you actually
do here on Earth,

00:29:26.928 --> 00:29:30.432
constrained by economics and engineering
and all the rest of it, right?

00:29:30.473 --> 00:29:33.059
And the answer is we can do
an incredible amount.

00:29:33.101 --> 00:29:36.521
Abundant, available
where you want it, when you want it.

00:29:36.563 --> 00:29:37.981
Everybody should have energy,

00:29:37.981 --> 00:29:41.318
including the three billion people
who use less electricity

00:29:41.359 --> 00:29:43.194
than my refrigerator uses.

00:29:43.737 --> 00:29:48.825
Well, the good news is, every day,
the Earth receives 163,000 terawatts

00:29:48.825 --> 00:29:50.660
of energy from the sun.

00:29:50.660 --> 00:29:52.662
About half of that bounces back to space,

00:29:52.704 --> 00:29:55.332
but about 80,000 terawatts
arrive at the Earth

00:29:55.373 --> 00:29:56.750
in a form we can use.

00:29:56.791 --> 00:30:00.253
For reference, today, the world uses
about 26 terawatts of energy,

00:30:00.253 --> 00:30:02.672
and we got more than solar and wind.

00:30:02.672 --> 00:30:05.133
We have geothermal,
we have hydro, we have nuclear.

00:30:05.133 --> 00:30:06.968
There’s other kinds of clean energies.

00:30:07.010 --> 00:30:11.222
And some of the best resources are,
in fact, in the Global South.

00:30:11.264 --> 00:30:14.768
These places are not simply
future climate victims.

00:30:14.809 --> 00:30:18.438
These places are latent
energy superpowers.

00:30:18.480 --> 00:30:20.649
My favorite example of this is Chile.

00:30:20.690 --> 00:30:22.984
Almost eight years ago now
their government said,

00:30:22.984 --> 00:30:26.154
wait a second, we can make
the cheapest green electricity on Earth.

00:30:26.196 --> 00:30:28.490
We have hydropower, we have solar power,

00:30:28.531 --> 00:30:32.160
we have wind power and the best resources
anywhere in the world.

00:30:32.160 --> 00:30:35.497
So let's start by making
a lot of green electricity.

00:30:35.538 --> 00:30:37.290
And then they said, because of that,

00:30:37.332 --> 00:30:40.210
we can also make green hydrogen
cheaper than anywhere else.

00:30:40.251 --> 00:30:44.547
And then Japan was like, hello,
we want your green hydrogen.

00:30:44.589 --> 00:30:45.965
Can you ship it to us?

00:30:46.341 --> 00:30:47.467
MZ: Is that possible?

00:30:47.509 --> 00:30:48.677
JF: Totally.

00:30:48.718 --> 00:30:52.180
And Japan is like, we will also give you
money to pay for these projects.

00:30:52.180 --> 00:30:54.391
We'll loan you money
to pay for these projects.

00:30:54.391 --> 00:30:56.351
Could you please use Japanese technology?

00:30:56.351 --> 00:30:58.812
So they're using Japanese
turbines, electrolyzers,

00:30:58.853 --> 00:31:01.773
they're using Japanese ships
to haul the ammonia around.

00:31:01.773 --> 00:31:03.775
So Japan gets rich on this,

00:31:03.775 --> 00:31:05.235
Chile gets rich on this.

00:31:05.235 --> 00:31:06.403
MZ: Win-win.

00:31:06.403 --> 00:31:07.570
JF: Totally win-win.

00:31:07.612 --> 00:31:09.030
MZ: Win-win-win, planet, too.

00:31:09.030 --> 00:31:11.991
JF: And that model, other countries
are looking at that, going,

00:31:12.033 --> 00:31:13.201
I want some of that.

00:31:13.201 --> 00:31:15.870
And that's why I like
this vision of abundance.

00:31:15.912 --> 00:31:20.750
15 years ago, solar
was the most expensive power.

00:31:20.750 --> 00:31:23.753
Now it is the cheapest
form of electricity, right?

00:31:23.753 --> 00:31:26.673
Which is astonishing,
it's totally amazing.

00:31:26.715 --> 00:31:29.342
And a whole bunch of people
are like, now we're done.

00:31:29.384 --> 00:31:32.303
And I'm like, no, we can do
that with everything.

00:31:32.762 --> 00:31:34.180
We can do that with wind.

00:31:34.180 --> 00:31:37.183
We can do that with nuclear,
we can do that with geothermal.

00:31:37.684 --> 00:31:42.439
But these projects take time,
money and people.

00:31:42.856 --> 00:31:44.607
We need to develop the human capital

00:31:44.607 --> 00:31:47.485
as part of these investment
projects for decades.

00:31:47.485 --> 00:31:49.738
And innovation,

00:31:49.779 --> 00:31:52.699
we need much more energy
in many places, much cheaper.

00:31:52.741 --> 00:31:54.492
That's an innovation agenda.

00:31:54.492 --> 00:31:57.162
For solar, that was
the United States, Germany, China

00:31:57.162 --> 00:31:58.955
and others acting together.

00:31:59.372 --> 00:32:02.292
Well, if that's the recipe,
we can do that again.

00:32:02.333 --> 00:32:06.087
We're already doing it with electric
vehicles and clean hydrogen production.

00:32:06.129 --> 00:32:09.090
We can certainly do it
by turning electricity into fuels.

00:32:09.090 --> 00:32:12.635
MZ: It sounds like the climate discussion
needs to be kind of like improv,

00:32:12.635 --> 00:32:14.679
that it's a "yes and" sort of thing.

00:32:14.679 --> 00:32:16.556
JF: I'm so glad you said that.

00:32:16.598 --> 00:32:19.517
So many people don't understand
my love of improv comedy.

00:32:19.517 --> 00:32:22.854
Yes, it is a “yes and” circumstance.

00:32:22.854 --> 00:32:25.690
Because mostly it is
about room for agreement.

00:32:25.732 --> 00:32:28.193
Where do you find the point
where everybody is like,

00:32:28.234 --> 00:32:29.360
I can agree on that?

00:32:29.402 --> 00:32:32.322
One of the things I love
about the infrastructure bill,

00:32:32.363 --> 00:32:35.158
one of the things I love
about the Inflation Reduction Act,

00:32:35.158 --> 00:32:37.160
neither of them had
the word climate in it.

00:32:37.202 --> 00:32:39.329
They were both massive
climate bills, right?

00:32:39.329 --> 00:32:42.373
That was a way to get everybody
on the same page.

00:32:42.415 --> 00:32:46.586
Nationwide, they basically tripled
the clean energy research budget.

00:32:46.628 --> 00:32:47.796
That's a good start.

00:32:47.837 --> 00:32:52.634
But that made it all
of one percent of GDP.

00:32:52.634 --> 00:32:56.471
And for comparison, we spend
twice that on pharmaceuticals.

00:32:56.513 --> 00:32:58.973
So I think it's fair to say
we should get going.

00:32:58.973 --> 00:33:00.767
We should spend a lot more money.

00:33:00.809 --> 00:33:02.435
We've done stuff like this before.

00:33:02.477 --> 00:33:05.021
We've done World War II,
we've done the Marshall Plan,

00:33:05.063 --> 00:33:06.606
like, we’ve done the space shot,

00:33:06.606 --> 00:33:09.484
like we actually know how to move
really quickly as a nation.

00:33:09.526 --> 00:33:10.693
We did this for COVID.

00:33:10.735 --> 00:33:11.903
Collective action,

00:33:11.903 --> 00:33:14.614
building together is what makes
the difficult possible

00:33:14.656 --> 00:33:18.284
and nourishes the soul
through mission and purpose.

00:33:18.910 --> 00:33:22.789
We know what to do and we can act,

00:33:22.789 --> 00:33:24.624
not out of anger or fear,

00:33:24.666 --> 00:33:27.502
but out of generosity and common purpose,

00:33:27.544 --> 00:33:30.046
bringing aspiration and humility together.

00:33:30.088 --> 00:33:33.591
We're going to build a thriving, vibrant,

00:33:33.633 --> 00:33:36.302
exciting world full of potential

00:33:36.302 --> 00:33:38.930
that's going to be built
on the back of infrastructure,

00:33:38.972 --> 00:33:40.640
innovation and investment

00:33:40.682 --> 00:33:44.644
that will harness the abundant,
sustainable, cheap energy

00:33:44.686 --> 00:33:47.272
that is our planet's endowment.

00:33:48.231 --> 00:33:49.399
Thank you.

00:33:49.399 --> 00:33:51.276
(Applause)

00:33:53.111 --> 00:33:56.406
[Chapter 4: A Just Transition]

00:33:56.447 --> 00:34:02.787
FD: There is a high risk
that when those exponential kick in,

00:34:02.829 --> 00:34:07.208
we see businesses running
after those opportunities.

00:34:07.250 --> 00:34:11.588
But then the distribution
of the benefits being very unequal.

00:34:11.629 --> 00:34:13.923
EZ: Many people like
discussing the importance

00:34:13.965 --> 00:34:16.009
of the just energy transition.

00:34:16.050 --> 00:34:19.554
And a lot of people don't understand
what justice really means.

00:34:19.596 --> 00:34:22.098
If we are to reach a revolution,

00:34:22.098 --> 00:34:24.392
a renewable energy future
has to be inclusive.

00:34:24.392 --> 00:34:25.602
AC: We have this push

00:34:25.602 --> 00:34:29.230
for what we're calling a just
and equitable energy transition.

00:34:29.230 --> 00:34:32.275
We need to make sure
that we are not exacerbating inequality,

00:34:32.275 --> 00:34:36.070
but rather we are ensuring
that that gap is closed

00:34:36.112 --> 00:34:40.283
and that the groups that have been
historically marginalized

00:34:40.325 --> 00:34:44.287
are not once again being
marginalized in a new way.

00:34:44.287 --> 00:34:48.708
NT: Most of emissions come
from wealthy, energy-guzzling citizens,

00:34:48.750 --> 00:34:51.169
but a lot of the economic benefit

00:34:51.169 --> 00:34:53.922
and human development benefit will come

00:34:53.963 --> 00:34:58.217
not from decarbonizing
high-carbon lives and lifestyles,

00:34:58.259 --> 00:35:00.720
but from providing clean,

00:35:00.720 --> 00:35:03.848
distributed energy and tools

00:35:03.848 --> 00:35:06.684
and solutions to those who don't
have access to any of them.

00:35:06.726 --> 00:35:11.064
LN: When we speak of countries
in need of economic development,

00:35:11.105 --> 00:35:14.108
we mostly speak of countries
that are hit by the climate crisis

00:35:14.150 --> 00:35:15.318
the hardest right now.

00:35:15.360 --> 00:35:18.780
It should be the biggest wish
and job and task

00:35:18.821 --> 00:35:23.034
for all those countries who have burned
so much fossil fuel already

00:35:23.076 --> 00:35:26.496
to support any other country
in the so-called Global South,

00:35:26.537 --> 00:35:29.540
to get an economy running
without fossil fuels,

00:35:29.582 --> 00:35:31.960
to not repeat all the mistakes
that we have made.

00:35:32.001 --> 00:35:36.297
HD: To ensure that the solar revolution
that has been seen around the world

00:35:36.297 --> 00:35:38.383
comes to places like Africa,

00:35:38.424 --> 00:35:42.929
we need to be really conscious
that the history and the lived experience

00:35:42.971 --> 00:35:46.391
when it comes to energy
is very different for poor countries.

00:35:46.432 --> 00:35:49.352
Rebekah Shirley: We love to remind
Global South communities,

00:35:49.352 --> 00:35:50.561
especially Africa,

00:35:50.561 --> 00:35:54.649
about the vast renewable energy potential
that they have, which is true.

00:35:54.691 --> 00:36:00.279
But the finance flows to deliver
on that potential remain very scarce.

00:36:00.321 --> 00:36:04.867
Africa, despite being 90 percent
of those across the entire globe

00:36:04.867 --> 00:36:07.328
that still do not have
access to electricity

00:36:07.370 --> 00:36:10.081
and being 17 percent
of the global population,

00:36:10.123 --> 00:36:13.042
we're actually two percent
of international finance.

00:36:13.042 --> 00:36:16.879
Money doesn't actually flow
to these spaces naturally,

00:36:16.921 --> 00:36:21.759
and we need to do a lot of work
to get global finance to these spaces.

00:36:23.136 --> 00:36:27.432
MZ: A world of abundant
clean energy for everyone.

00:36:27.473 --> 00:36:28.891
That is the goal.

00:36:29.308 --> 00:36:31.561
But what about in the short term?

00:36:31.561 --> 00:36:32.770
Take Detroit.

00:36:32.770 --> 00:36:36.441
This once-wealthy city has been through
some seriously hard times.

00:36:36.441 --> 00:36:37.942
How is it rebuilding itself

00:36:37.984 --> 00:36:40.236
so that everyone here can thrive

00:36:40.236 --> 00:36:43.114
while dealing with extreme heat, flooding,

00:36:43.156 --> 00:36:45.116
and other climate problems?

00:36:45.116 --> 00:36:49.704
Anika Goss is looking
to Detroit's past to plan its future.

00:36:51.456 --> 00:36:54.250
AG: I am a third-generation Detroiter.

00:36:55.084 --> 00:36:59.380
My grandmother moved to Detroit in 1936

00:36:59.380 --> 00:37:01.299
during the Great Migration

00:37:01.299 --> 00:37:05.261
and brought all
of her southern ways with her.

00:37:05.303 --> 00:37:06.679
She owned a home

00:37:06.679 --> 00:37:09.932
and knew that home ownership
would create wealth

00:37:09.932 --> 00:37:14.103
and opportunity for her growing family.

00:37:14.103 --> 00:37:16.564
Up until the late 1950s,

00:37:16.606 --> 00:37:20.485
Detroit was a haven
for middle-class families

00:37:20.526 --> 00:37:24.697
living in neighborhoods
where there was green space

00:37:24.739 --> 00:37:28.743
and community connectivity
and opportunity.

00:37:28.785 --> 00:37:33.081
My grandmother,
she had this amazing garden,

00:37:33.081 --> 00:37:35.333
and it was just abundant.

00:37:35.333 --> 00:37:39.420
There were all kinds of flowers and food.

00:37:39.462 --> 00:37:42.715
You know, she grew vegetables back there,

00:37:42.757 --> 00:37:46.094
and it was just a magical
place for a little girl.

00:37:46.094 --> 00:37:50.306
MZ: I mean, what you're describing
is a Detroit that, yes, has huge problems,

00:37:50.348 --> 00:37:51.641
segregation.

00:37:51.682 --> 00:37:55.478
But at the same time, a woman,

00:37:55.520 --> 00:37:56.687
she can have a job,

00:37:56.687 --> 00:37:58.981
she can own a house,
she can raise her family,

00:37:59.023 --> 00:38:01.818
she can have fresh fruits and vegetables.

00:38:01.859 --> 00:38:07.031
AG: It was certainly a place where
you could gain economic opportunity

00:38:07.031 --> 00:38:09.117
faster than you can now.

00:38:09.659 --> 00:38:14.330
My grandmother's Detroit
is not the Detroit that I live in today.

00:38:14.330 --> 00:38:17.542
All of those sites where
there was manufacturing

00:38:17.542 --> 00:38:23.005
and industrial sites that led
to our economic boom,

00:38:23.005 --> 00:38:25.591
many stand vacant and abandoned.

00:38:25.633 --> 00:38:29.262
These industrial sites have led

00:38:29.303 --> 00:38:33.182
to dangerous contamination

00:38:33.182 --> 00:38:37.353
to our land and our water and our air.

00:38:37.395 --> 00:38:39.188
MZ: So, just lay it out for us.

00:38:39.188 --> 00:38:41.399
For people who maybe don't see,

00:38:41.440 --> 00:38:46.696
like, how is there a link between
economic inequality and climate change?

00:38:46.737 --> 00:38:50.992
AG: You know, I'm so surprised
that it feels like separate problems.

00:38:50.992 --> 00:38:53.536
But I'm also really glad
that people are asking.

00:38:53.953 --> 00:38:57.290
Because if you're living around

00:38:57.331 --> 00:39:02.420
and working in a community
that is low-income,

00:39:02.461 --> 00:39:04.714
where there are fewer jobs,

00:39:04.714 --> 00:39:07.300
the housing is deteriorated,

00:39:07.300 --> 00:39:13.181
this is a neighborhood that's also
more likely to be closer to a factory

00:39:13.181 --> 00:39:14.974
that's emitting pollutants,

00:39:14.974 --> 00:39:18.686
likely to have water that's contaminated,

00:39:18.728 --> 00:39:23.900
probably has fewer parks
and fewer intentional green spaces.

00:39:23.941 --> 00:39:27.278
The people who are living
in those communities

00:39:27.320 --> 00:39:30.698
are at risk for climate impacts first.

00:39:30.698 --> 00:39:34.660
So we have to do something
in those neighborhoods right away.

00:39:36.370 --> 00:39:39.332
MZ: So what can this healthier
future look like?

00:39:39.332 --> 00:39:41.375
Anika showed me some
of the work she's done

00:39:41.417 --> 00:39:44.295
in partnership with different
neighborhoods in Detroit,

00:39:44.295 --> 00:39:49.091
to make them both
more enjoyable and more resilient.

00:39:49.091 --> 00:39:51.510
First, she took me to the Circle Forest.

00:39:51.552 --> 00:39:54.680
It's an oasis right
in the middle of the city.

00:39:55.389 --> 00:39:58.226
AG: This is six lots that were vacant,

00:39:58.267 --> 00:40:02.271
and we all worked together
with this community to build a forest.

00:40:02.313 --> 00:40:04.190
MZ: Beautiful and a great example

00:40:04.190 --> 00:40:06.442
of how nonprofits can work
with communities

00:40:06.484 --> 00:40:10.321
to transform them into sustainable,
welcoming places.

00:40:10.780 --> 00:40:14.242
AG: We might have a good idea
about gardens and tree planting,

00:40:14.283 --> 00:40:16.077
but this is where people live.

00:40:16.118 --> 00:40:18.079
And so you ask first.

00:40:18.120 --> 00:40:19.830
This is a new effort,

00:40:19.872 --> 00:40:23.834
of this idea of a resilient
neighborhood or calling it that.

00:40:23.876 --> 00:40:27.463
So these are large tracts of vacancy

00:40:27.463 --> 00:40:33.094
that can be used to provide
environmental sustainability,

00:40:33.135 --> 00:40:36.597
community resilience and beauty.

00:40:37.515 --> 00:40:38.975
Watch for the poison ivy.

00:40:39.016 --> 00:40:40.518
It's not pretend.

00:40:40.559 --> 00:40:42.270
(Laughter)

00:40:44.272 --> 00:40:46.607
MZ: Next we met up with Katrina Watkins,

00:40:46.607 --> 00:40:51.404
the founder and CEO of the Bailey Park
Neighborhood Development Corporation,

00:40:51.445 --> 00:40:56.617
a group revitalizing this historic area
by turning vacant lots into parks

00:40:56.659 --> 00:40:58.995
that can stand up to extreme weather.

00:40:59.495 --> 00:41:02.748
Katrina Watkins: My family has been
in this neighborhood since 1946.

00:41:03.124 --> 00:41:05.543
I grew up hearing the stories from my dad,

00:41:05.584 --> 00:41:11.549
him telling me about how people thought
it was a ghetto, but it wasn't.

00:41:11.882 --> 00:41:15.720
Those were families, and people
worked and had businesses,

00:41:15.761 --> 00:41:18.014
and people looked out for each other.

00:41:18.014 --> 00:41:20.808
And it was those type of stories
that made me say,

00:41:20.850 --> 00:41:24.895
wow, we just really need
to bring that back.

00:41:24.937 --> 00:41:28.441
MZ: So then briefly fill us in
about how things changed.

00:41:28.482 --> 00:41:30.609
KW: Things deteriorated
in this neighborhood.

00:41:30.609 --> 00:41:32.278
The homes got torn down,

00:41:32.320 --> 00:41:36.073
left just a lot of vacant lots,
a lot of overgrowth.

00:41:36.115 --> 00:41:37.616
It wasn't safe.

00:41:37.658 --> 00:41:39.910
I would see my little nieces and nephews.

00:41:39.910 --> 00:41:42.496
They would just be
on their little skateboards,

00:41:42.496 --> 00:41:44.415
you know, not paying attention,

00:41:44.457 --> 00:41:46.083
the stop sign is covered.

00:41:46.083 --> 00:41:48.794
And me and my dad was like,
we just have to do something.

00:41:48.794 --> 00:41:50.880
So we started out as Bailey Park Project,

00:41:50.921 --> 00:41:55.051
focusing on the park and green
infrastructure and trees and flowers.

00:41:55.092 --> 00:41:57.219
And then over the course of the years,

00:41:57.261 --> 00:41:59.472
we started doing
more community development.

00:41:59.513 --> 00:42:01.974
MZ: I wonder, like,
how do you describe it to people

00:42:01.974 --> 00:42:03.768
when you explain what's happening,

00:42:03.809 --> 00:42:06.020
what you're trying to do
for your neighborhood,

00:42:06.020 --> 00:42:09.273
the changes that you're making
and how it relates to climate change?

00:42:09.273 --> 00:42:11.108
KW: One really great example,

00:42:11.150 --> 00:42:13.819
when people come to the park,
like right now,

00:42:13.861 --> 00:42:17.114
this is like, literally a heat island

00:42:17.114 --> 00:42:18.824
because our trees haven't grown.

00:42:18.866 --> 00:42:20.493
So it is very hot.

00:42:20.493 --> 00:42:23.329
So sometimes it's so hot
that the kids don't come out

00:42:23.371 --> 00:42:25.206
and play until the evening.

00:42:25.247 --> 00:42:28.000
That education of knowing
the benefit of trees

00:42:28.042 --> 00:42:30.878
and cooling down an environment,

00:42:30.920 --> 00:42:32.588
how it makes the air cleaner.

00:42:32.630 --> 00:42:35.341
There's a lot of people
that suffer from asthma.

00:42:35.383 --> 00:42:36.884
So trees can make a difference.

00:42:36.884 --> 00:42:39.053
And if you go to places
like Grosse Pointe,

00:42:39.053 --> 00:42:41.847
you will see this beautiful
tree canopy down.

00:42:41.847 --> 00:42:44.809
Like their homes are probably
20 degrees cooler

00:42:44.850 --> 00:42:46.435
you know, than our homes,

00:42:46.477 --> 00:42:48.437
AG: Having a lot of green space

00:42:48.479 --> 00:42:53.526
actually contributes
to the climate resiliency

00:42:53.526 --> 00:42:54.944
for this neighborhood.

00:42:54.944 --> 00:42:59.990
So all of this grass actually
can manage stormwater better.

00:43:00.032 --> 00:43:05.079
The idea of leading new development
in this neighborhood

00:43:05.079 --> 00:43:07.081
with green,

00:43:07.123 --> 00:43:08.457
what it says to me

00:43:08.499 --> 00:43:12.336
is that the residents
are really envisioning

00:43:12.378 --> 00:43:14.922
the neighborhood
that they want to live in.

00:43:14.922 --> 00:43:17.883
And that I just -- I'm really
excited about that.

00:43:18.717 --> 00:43:23.347
MZ: Turning great loss and upheaval
into even greater opportunities.

00:43:23.347 --> 00:43:27.435
These Detroiters are creating
a blueprint for an urban future

00:43:27.476 --> 00:43:29.478
that every city can learn from.

00:43:32.773 --> 00:43:36.444
[Chapter 5: Agriculture]

00:43:38.571 --> 00:43:42.616
TED-Ed: About 10,000 years ago,
humans began to farm.

00:43:43.075 --> 00:43:47.455
This agricultural revolution
was a turning point in our history

00:43:47.496 --> 00:43:51.375
that enabled people to settle,
build and create.

00:43:51.709 --> 00:43:57.298
In short, agriculture
enabled the existence of civilization.

00:43:57.339 --> 00:44:02.428
Today, approximately 40 percent
of our planet is farmland.

00:44:02.470 --> 00:44:04.305
Spread all over the world,

00:44:04.346 --> 00:44:08.142
these agricultural lands
are the pieces to a global puzzle

00:44:08.142 --> 00:44:09.810
we are all facing.

00:44:09.852 --> 00:44:11.061
In the future,

00:44:11.061 --> 00:44:16.984
how can we feed every member
of a growing population a healthy diet?

00:44:17.443 --> 00:44:19.111
Meeting this goal will require

00:44:19.153 --> 00:44:23.240
nothing short of a second
agricultural revolution.

00:44:23.282 --> 00:44:25.284
The first agricultural revolution

00:44:25.326 --> 00:44:29.288
was characterized
by expansion and exploitation,

00:44:29.330 --> 00:44:32.208
feeding people at the expense of forests,

00:44:32.249 --> 00:44:34.210
wildlife and water,

00:44:34.210 --> 00:44:37.546
and destabilizing the climate
in the process.

00:44:38.047 --> 00:44:40.925
That's not an option the next time around.

00:44:41.550 --> 00:44:44.637
Agriculture depends on a stable climate

00:44:44.678 --> 00:44:47.640
with predictable seasons
and weather patterns.

00:44:47.640 --> 00:44:51.685
This means we can't keep
expanding our agricultural lands,

00:44:51.685 --> 00:44:54.980
because doing so will undermine
the environmental conditions

00:44:55.022 --> 00:44:58.067
that make agriculture possible
in the first place.

00:44:58.817 --> 00:45:01.779
Instead, the next agricultural revolution

00:45:01.779 --> 00:45:06.242
will have to increase the output
of our existing farmland for the long term

00:45:06.283 --> 00:45:10.037
while protecting biodiversity,
conserving water,

00:45:10.079 --> 00:45:13.666
and reducing pollution
and greenhouse gas emissions.

00:45:14.959 --> 00:45:17.836
So what will the future farms look like?

00:45:18.963 --> 00:45:21.840
In Bangladesh, Cambodia and Nepal,

00:45:21.840 --> 00:45:23.717
new approaches to rice production

00:45:23.759 --> 00:45:27.763
may dramatically decrease
greenhouse gas emissions in the future.

00:45:27.763 --> 00:45:31.350
Rice is a staple food
for three billion people

00:45:31.392 --> 00:45:35.062
and the main source of livelihood
for millions of households.

00:45:35.104 --> 00:45:39.441
More than 90 percent of rice
is grown in flooded paddies,

00:45:39.483 --> 00:45:41.569
which use a lot of water

00:45:41.569 --> 00:45:45.281
and release 11 percent
of annual methane emissions,

00:45:45.322 --> 00:45:47.449
which accounts for one to two percent

00:45:47.491 --> 00:45:51.328
of total annual greenhouse
gas emissions globally.

00:45:51.870 --> 00:45:56.208
By experimenting with new strains
of rice, irrigating less

00:45:56.250 --> 00:46:00.212
and adopting less labor-intensive
ways of planting seeds,

00:46:00.254 --> 00:46:04.174
farmers in these countries have already
increased their incomes

00:46:04.216 --> 00:46:05.384
and crop yields

00:46:05.384 --> 00:46:08.679
while cutting down
on greenhouse gas emissions.

00:46:11.473 --> 00:46:15.227
MZ: Related technologies
are also taking root in the US.

00:46:15.227 --> 00:46:17.896
Father and daughter farmers,
Jim and Jessica Whitaker,

00:46:17.938 --> 00:46:20.941
have come up with their own
strategies to reduce methane

00:46:20.941 --> 00:46:24.111
that are working
on their farm in Arkansas.

00:46:24.153 --> 00:46:29.116
But convincing everyone else to change
is their next big challenge.

00:46:29.116 --> 00:46:31.744
So let's go back to how you got to be

00:46:31.785 --> 00:46:34.580
such a big supplier of rice
in this country.

00:46:34.580 --> 00:46:38.292
You tell a story about how you
were 22 and you were like,

00:46:38.334 --> 00:46:41.378
I'm going to start my own farm, but --

00:46:42.546 --> 00:46:43.672
not that easy.

00:46:43.714 --> 00:46:45.174
Jim Whitaker: No, it's not.

00:46:45.174 --> 00:46:48.802
Let me tell you something about renting
a farm when you're 22 years old.

00:46:49.511 --> 00:46:54.475
No one rents you a farm
unless no one else wants to rent it.

00:46:54.516 --> 00:46:57.645
(Laughter)

00:46:57.645 --> 00:47:00.189
It was a big piece of land,
it was cash rent,

00:47:00.230 --> 00:47:02.733
I mean, the stakes were set.

00:47:03.275 --> 00:47:05.319
We were doomed to fail.

00:47:05.653 --> 00:47:08.822
And we weren't focused on environmental
sustainability back then.

00:47:08.864 --> 00:47:11.450
We were focused
on economic sustainability.

00:47:11.492 --> 00:47:12.868
How do we make higher yield?

00:47:12.868 --> 00:47:15.079
How do we use less fertilizer?

00:47:15.079 --> 00:47:17.915
How do we get to the next year
and feed our family?

00:47:18.832 --> 00:47:22.127
It happens that economic
and environmental sustainability

00:47:22.169 --> 00:47:25.130
go hand in hand along
with social sustainability.

00:47:25.130 --> 00:47:28.384
As we used less fertilizer,
used less water,

00:47:28.425 --> 00:47:30.219
our yield started going up.

00:47:30.219 --> 00:47:33.138
MZ: Can you spell out for me
what the problem is with rice

00:47:33.180 --> 00:47:35.099
when it comes to the environment?

00:47:35.140 --> 00:47:37.184
JW: Soil is full of microbes.

00:47:37.226 --> 00:47:39.687
And what they're doing
in the soil on a basic level

00:47:39.728 --> 00:47:41.563
is they're down there chewing away,

00:47:41.605 --> 00:47:45.275
eating up the biomass
that's left over from the crop before.

00:47:45.317 --> 00:47:48.445
And out of that comes methane,

00:47:48.487 --> 00:47:52.574
the same way it happens in cattle
operations or whatever.

00:47:52.616 --> 00:47:55.077
MZ: But you decided
to do it a different way.

00:47:55.119 --> 00:47:56.995
JW: We do it a little bit differently.

00:47:56.995 --> 00:47:59.164
Arkansas is a very water-rich state,

00:47:59.206 --> 00:48:01.083
but we have a dry season,

00:48:01.125 --> 00:48:03.335
right in the middle
of summer is a dry season.

00:48:03.335 --> 00:48:04.712
So when we dry the soil,

00:48:04.712 --> 00:48:08.340
that anaerobic microbial
that's living in that soup,

00:48:08.382 --> 00:48:12.636
that mushy soil,
either dies or goes dormant.

00:48:12.678 --> 00:48:15.389
So we break his life cycle.

00:48:15.389 --> 00:48:16.557
MZ: Oh!

00:48:16.598 --> 00:48:19.226
JW: And then it stops emitting methane.

00:48:19.226 --> 00:48:20.644
But it doesn't hurt the rice.

00:48:20.644 --> 00:48:23.147
That actually helps rice,
when you use less water.

00:48:23.188 --> 00:48:26.859
We have less runoff, less erosion,
less nutrients leaving our field.

00:48:26.859 --> 00:48:29.445
Nothing leaves our field
unless we want it to.

00:48:29.486 --> 00:48:31.780
Jessica Whitaker Allen:
We started tracking this.

00:48:31.822 --> 00:48:34.158
We saw that our water use
was down and we thought,

00:48:34.199 --> 00:48:37.494
OK, well, we need to add to that,
let's add more data points to that.

00:48:37.494 --> 00:48:41.165
Let's add our fertilizer,
let's add our fuel usage.

00:48:41.206 --> 00:48:45.586
And so we really started recording
all this stuff by hand

00:48:45.586 --> 00:48:47.546
in just an Excel spreadsheet.

00:48:47.588 --> 00:48:49.381
We didn't know what we were doing.

00:48:49.423 --> 00:48:51.425
MZ: But you knew you were on to something.

00:48:51.467 --> 00:48:53.677
JWA: Yes, so we started
recording everything.

00:48:53.719 --> 00:48:57.765
MZ: Do you remember if there was a moment
when the two of you thought, like,

00:48:57.806 --> 00:49:01.101
what we're doing,
it shouldn't be special to us.

00:49:01.101 --> 00:49:02.436
Why aren't more people --

00:49:02.436 --> 00:49:05.355
JW: To collect the data,
have the equipment

00:49:05.397 --> 00:49:07.065
and the cloud-based technology

00:49:07.107 --> 00:49:10.110
and the stuff in the field to record it,

00:49:10.152 --> 00:49:15.449
it’s just massively time-consuming,
and consumers don’t pay for it.

00:49:15.949 --> 00:49:19.119
JWA: Well and people like us,
why would you want to do it?

00:49:19.119 --> 00:49:22.623
I mean, take a walk
through your supermarket.

00:49:22.664 --> 00:49:25.459
Everything’s sustainable,
everything’s greenwashed.

00:49:25.501 --> 00:49:27.669
You can put whatever
you want to on a package,

00:49:27.669 --> 00:49:29.755
there's no rules, there's no standards.

00:49:29.755 --> 00:49:32.299
So we're trying to make that standard.

00:49:32.341 --> 00:49:34.760
We live and work in southeast Arkansas.

00:49:34.760 --> 00:49:37.971
Our town has 4,000 residents
and one stoplight.

00:49:38.430 --> 00:49:42.059
The nearest airport, Starbucks
shopping mall, Whole Foods,

00:49:42.100 --> 00:49:44.436
is two hours away in any direction.

00:49:45.437 --> 00:49:49.024
Without places like this
and farmers like us,

00:49:49.066 --> 00:49:51.318
you'd be hungry, naked and sober.

00:49:51.819 --> 00:49:54.988
(Laughter)

00:49:55.030 --> 00:49:57.449
I'm a farmer, but not in the way
you might think.

00:49:57.449 --> 00:50:00.452
I don’t drive the tractors,
and I don’t plant the rice.

00:50:00.452 --> 00:50:03.622
But I know how to take what my dad
has learned and what he is doing

00:50:03.664 --> 00:50:04.957
and share that with others.

00:50:04.957 --> 00:50:08.752
We are going to work with farmers
in southeast Arkansas

00:50:08.752 --> 00:50:10.963
to educate them about the benefits

00:50:11.004 --> 00:50:12.756
of growing sustainable rice.

00:50:12.756 --> 00:50:14.716
JW: Let me tell you why that's important.

00:50:14.758 --> 00:50:17.177
There are 400 million acres
of rice grown globally.

00:50:17.219 --> 00:50:20.597
And our methods, if used, can reduce
greenhouse gas by 50 percent,

00:50:20.639 --> 00:50:22.808
reduce water use by 50 percent,

00:50:22.808 --> 00:50:25.102
increase yields to feed a hungry world.

00:50:25.853 --> 00:50:28.105
(Applause)

00:50:28.146 --> 00:50:33.902
MZ: We hear a lot about how weather
has gotten more extreme, flooding, heat.

00:50:33.944 --> 00:50:35.529
What's it been like in Arkansas?

00:50:35.529 --> 00:50:40.284
JW: So in 2016, we had a 500-year flood.

00:50:41.076 --> 00:50:42.202
We were ...

00:50:49.209 --> 00:50:53.213
We get a picture of our place flooded.

00:50:53.213 --> 00:50:54.631
14 inches of rain.

00:50:54.673 --> 00:50:56.425
That was a 500-year flood.

00:50:58.176 --> 00:50:59.720
Hurt a lot of people.

00:50:59.761 --> 00:51:02.264
And then we had a 1,000-year flood.

00:51:02.264 --> 00:51:04.850
And we lost so many acres.

00:51:04.850 --> 00:51:08.103
And you know, when houses
get flooded in rural communities,

00:51:08.145 --> 00:51:09.730
they don't come back.

00:51:09.730 --> 00:51:12.900
I mean, when you have
whole neighborhoods get flooded,

00:51:12.900 --> 00:51:15.068
they don't rebuild them,
they don't come back.

00:51:15.068 --> 00:51:17.946
JWA: I feel like almost every day
I'm getting a notification

00:51:17.946 --> 00:51:20.449
from the weather Channel
of an excessive heat warning

00:51:20.490 --> 00:51:23.076
or thunderstorm warning or hail threat.

00:51:23.076 --> 00:51:26.622
You know, you don't really know
what you're going to expect.

00:51:26.663 --> 00:51:30.125
MZ: When you think
about what it will be like,

00:51:30.167 --> 00:51:32.085
I don’t know, five years, 10 years,

00:51:32.127 --> 00:51:35.213
for someone to go into the supermarket
and think, oh, right,

00:51:35.213 --> 00:51:38.383
I need to get rice off my grocery list.

00:51:38.383 --> 00:51:41.970
What ideally would you like
to see on the shelf there?

00:51:42.763 --> 00:51:45.933
JW: So I think it’s going to change,

00:51:45.933 --> 00:51:49.895
because I like to study
what companies want.

00:51:49.937 --> 00:51:53.607
I mean, if you figure out what they want,
then you know what to give them.

00:51:53.607 --> 00:51:56.944
And when you read
their sustainability goals,

00:51:56.985 --> 00:51:59.529
they're all saying
they're going to be net zero.

00:51:59.529 --> 00:52:03.241
The only way they can be net zero
is they bring the farmer into the loop.

00:52:03.283 --> 00:52:05.035
So if we're able to do this,

00:52:05.077 --> 00:52:08.538
in five years, everybody's
doing these practices,

00:52:08.580 --> 00:52:10.666
the US rice industry

00:52:10.707 --> 00:52:13.919
will be the most sustainable
industry in the world.

00:52:15.629 --> 00:52:16.964
MZ: Can you do it?

00:52:17.005 --> 00:52:18.382
JW: Oh, yeah.

00:52:18.423 --> 00:52:19.925
It's about to happen.

00:52:21.176 --> 00:52:23.512
MZ: You've just heard a lot of ideas,

00:52:23.553 --> 00:52:27.099
a lot of stories and a lot of big plans.

00:52:27.599 --> 00:52:29.351
So we want to wrap things up

00:52:29.351 --> 00:52:31.228
by asking our experts:

00:52:31.228 --> 00:52:34.606
What if we actually achieved
our climate goals?

00:52:34.648 --> 00:52:36.900
What would that future even look like?

00:52:37.317 --> 00:52:39.695
They, of course, had a lot of thoughts.

00:52:39.736 --> 00:52:41.446
We'll let you pick your favorites.

00:52:41.446 --> 00:52:42.698
Thanks for watching.

00:52:44.074 --> 00:52:47.536
NT: If we are successful
in addressing the climate crisis,

00:52:47.577 --> 00:52:51.623
we massively reduce the vulnerability
and improve the economic

00:52:51.623 --> 00:52:55.919
and the creative prospects
of billions of people around the world

00:52:55.961 --> 00:52:58.422
who currently are basically excluded

00:52:58.422 --> 00:53:00.716
from all of the opportunities
which we have,

00:53:00.757 --> 00:53:04.720
which come from having abundant material
and energy all around us.

00:53:05.345 --> 00:53:06.513
That's the real prize.

00:53:06.555 --> 00:53:10.100
FD: A world where
we have had that exponential

00:53:10.142 --> 00:53:16.523
would be a world in which we have
clean, cheap energy for all.

00:53:16.523 --> 00:53:18.025
KB: At the end of this decade,

00:53:18.025 --> 00:53:21.528
the International Energy Agency believes
that we will have enough capacity

00:53:21.570 --> 00:53:23.238
to be producing 10 billion,

00:53:23.280 --> 00:53:25.574
10 billion batteries a year.

00:53:25.615 --> 00:53:29.661
It's enough for every person
on the planet to have their own battery.

00:53:29.703 --> 00:53:33.749
AC: I'm constantly surrounded
by young people just like me

00:53:33.749 --> 00:53:38.211
who are really, really, really passionate
about the world that we live in.

00:53:38.211 --> 00:53:39.379
For obvious reasons.

00:53:39.421 --> 00:53:41.173
It's the world that we will inherit.

00:53:41.173 --> 00:53:43.759
EZ: Women are very important
for the energy transition.

00:53:43.759 --> 00:53:46.470
So if we're talking about a world
where we have, you know,

00:53:46.511 --> 00:53:50.140
where we tackle climate change,
all I want to see is, you know,

00:53:50.140 --> 00:53:51.516
women in power.

00:53:51.558 --> 00:53:55.437
I want to see more solutions
being driven by young people.

00:53:55.437 --> 00:54:00.609
And I want our politicians to be truly
and meaningfully engaged in making change.

00:54:00.609 --> 00:54:06.323
NG: We always tend to underestimate
the pace of ultimate adoption and change.

00:54:06.323 --> 00:54:10.160
Today, there are more than 150 pathways,

00:54:10.202 --> 00:54:14.247
scientific pathways that we could
take to limit global warming.

00:54:14.247 --> 00:54:17.000
Al Gore: If we get to true net zero,

00:54:17.042 --> 00:54:21.838
astonishingly, global temperatures
will stop going up

00:54:21.880 --> 00:54:24.633
with a lag time of as little
as three to five years.

00:54:24.633 --> 00:54:27.302
Colin Averill: We've been able
to accelerate tree growth

00:54:27.344 --> 00:54:30.555
and carbon capture
and tree stems by 30 to 70 percent.

00:54:30.597 --> 00:54:33.350
Dan Jørgensen: Offshore wind
has the potential

00:54:33.391 --> 00:54:36.978
to cover the current electricity
demand of the entire world

00:54:36.978 --> 00:54:40.315
not once, not twice,

00:54:40.357 --> 00:54:41.650
18 times.

00:54:41.650 --> 00:54:45.445
NG: We need to decarbonize
the old economy that we have

00:54:45.487 --> 00:54:49.407
and invest to create
the new economy that we need

00:54:49.407 --> 00:54:53.703
Paul Hawken: Regenerative farming
is fairly simple in concept.

00:54:53.745 --> 00:54:57.249
It means creating
the conditions for more life.

00:54:57.290 --> 00:54:59.835
Josephine Phillips: We need
to buy less stuff,

00:54:59.835 --> 00:55:02.254
and we need to look after what we buy.

00:55:02.295 --> 00:55:06.675
Valuing the things that we own
is a climate solution.

00:55:06.675 --> 00:55:10.303
Vishaan Chakrabarti: We can all live
in this transit-rich, carbon-negative,

00:55:10.303 --> 00:55:11.805
affordable way

00:55:11.847 --> 00:55:15.809
and leave the vast majority
of the planet for nature,

00:55:15.851 --> 00:55:17.894
for agriculture, for clean oceans.

00:55:17.936 --> 00:55:19.146
We can do this.

00:55:19.187 --> 00:55:21.857
Susan Ruffo: The ocean is
a powerful source of solutions

00:55:21.857 --> 00:55:23.984
that we've overlooked for far too long.

00:55:24.025 --> 00:55:25.777
Al Roker: You have to be engaged.

00:55:25.819 --> 00:55:31.116
Let your elected officials know
that this is important to you.

00:55:31.116 --> 00:55:32.284
You have to vote,

00:55:32.325 --> 00:55:34.327
you have to go out there

00:55:34.369 --> 00:55:39.749
and support politicians
who are going to support our planet.

00:55:39.791 --> 00:55:43.670
Peggy Shepard: We can create
a legacy of environmental quality

00:55:43.712 --> 00:55:45.672
and climate resilience for all.

00:55:45.714 --> 00:55:47.799
Fehinti Balogun: You are not powerless

00:55:47.841 --> 00:55:53.263
that the "oh, there's nothing we can do,
just keep going" is symptom,

00:55:53.263 --> 00:55:54.681
not cure.

00:55:55.473 --> 00:55:56.892
But you are needed.

00:55:56.933 --> 00:55:59.519
Avinash Persaud: The power
of what we can do

00:55:59.519 --> 00:56:02.647
when it matters to us is unlimited.

00:56:02.647 --> 00:56:06.276
CF: It's not that we're
simply going to avoid

00:56:06.318 --> 00:56:08.278
the worst impacts of climate change.

00:56:08.320 --> 00:56:13.825
It’s that we’re going to improve
the quality of life for humans,

00:56:13.825 --> 00:56:17.037
both in the urban and in the rural sector,

00:56:17.078 --> 00:56:18.788
and for non-humans.

